declare(strict_types=1);
echo '<div class="mk-prose">';
echo '<p class="mk-muted" style="margin-top:0;">Western esoteric traditions in depth — Hermeticism, Gnosticism, Kabbalah, Neoplatonism, Alchemy, Rosicrucianism, Freemasonry, Theosophy, and Thelema.</p>';
echo '<p>The Western esoteric traditions constitute one of the most sophisticated and least understood bodies of knowledge in human history. Dismissed as superstition by the Enlightenment, ignored by mainstream religious institutions, and sensationalized by popular culture, they represent a continuous philosophical and spiritual tradition stretching from late antiquity to the present — a tradition that has shaped Renaissance art, modern psychology, and the vocabulary of contemporary spirituality more deeply than is generally acknowledged.</p>';
echo '<p>What unifies these traditions, despite their diversity, is a set of shared assumptions: that reality has hidden dimensions accessible to those who seek them; that the human being is more than a physical body and participates in a larger cosmic order; that knowledge of this order can be attained through study, practice, and initiation; and that such knowledge transforms the knower. These assumptions distinguish esoteric traditions from both mainstream religion (which emphasizes faith and community observance) and mainstream philosophy (which emphasizes rational argument).</p>';

// HERMETICISM
echo '<hr style="margin:28px 0;border:none;border-top:2px solid #e5e7eb;">';
echo '<h2 style="color:#b7791f;">&#x2295; Hermeticism</h2>';
echo '<p><strong>Origin:</strong> Egypt/Alexandria | <strong>Period:</strong> c. 100&ndash;300 CE (texts), claiming ancient Egyptian origin | <strong>Theism:</strong> Monotheistic emanationist | <strong>Proselytism:</strong> None &mdash; transmitted to seekers | <strong>Apostasy:</strong> Not applicable</p>';
echo '<h3>Origin and History</h3>';
echo '<p>Hermeticism derives from the <em>Corpus Hermeticum</em> &mdash; a collection of Greek texts attributed to Hermes Trismegistus ("Thrice-Greatest Hermes"), a legendary figure identified as a synthesis of the Greek god Hermes and the Egyptian god Thoth. Modern scholarship dates these texts to the 1st&ndash;3rd centuries CE, produced in Alexandria where Greek philosophy and Egyptian religious thought were in active dialogue. Renaissance scholars believed them to be ancient Egyptian wisdom older than Plato &mdash; this belief, though historically incorrect, drove their enormous influence on Renaissance magic and philosophy.</p>';
echo '<h3>Core Doctrine</h3>';
echo '<ul>';
echo '<li><strong>The Seven Hermetic Principles</strong> (from the <em>Kybalion</em>, 1908):</li>';
echo '<li style="list-style:none;padding-left:20px;">1. <strong>Mentalism</strong> &mdash; "The All is Mind; the Universe is Mental."</li>';
echo '<li style="list-style:none;padding-left:20px;">2. <strong>Correspondence</strong> &mdash; "As above, so below; as below, so above."</li>';
echo '<li style="list-style:none;padding-left:20px;">3. <strong>Vibration</strong> &mdash; "Nothing rests; everything moves; everything vibrates."</li>';
echo '<li style="list-style:none;padding-left:20px;">4. <strong>Polarity</strong> &mdash; "Everything is Dual; everything has poles."</li>';
echo '<li style="list-style:none;padding-left:20px;">5. <strong>Rhythm</strong> &mdash; "Everything flows, out and in; everything has its tides."</li>';
echo '<li style="list-style:none;padding-left:20px;">6. <strong>Cause and Effect</strong> &mdash; "Every Cause has its Effect; every Effect has its Cause."</li>';
echo '<li style="list-style:none;padding-left:20px;">7. <strong>Gender</strong> &mdash; "Gender is in everything; everything has its Masculine and Feminine Principles."</li>';
echo '<li><strong>The Emerald Tablet</strong> &mdash; the most concentrated expression of Hermetic philosophy: "That which is above is like that which is below." Source text for all subsequent alchemy.</li>';
echo '<li><strong>Reincarnation</strong> &mdash; the <em>Corpus Hermeticum</em> teaches that the soul descends through planetary spheres into matter, acquiring their qualities, and ascends back through the same spheres toward the divine &mdash; shedding those qualities at each level until it returns to the Source pure.</li>';
echo '</ul>';
echo '<h3>Key Text</h3>';
echo '<p><strong>Corpus Hermeticum</strong> (c. 100&ndash;300 CE). Free: <a href="https://sacred-texts.com/egy/herm/index.htm" target="_blank" rel="noopener">sacred-texts.com/egy/herm</a></p>';

// GNOSTICISM
echo '<hr style="margin:28px 0;border:none;border-top:2px solid #e5e7eb;">';
echo '<h2 style="color:#276749;">&#x2295; Gnosticism</h2>';
echo '<p><strong>Origin:</strong> Syria/Egypt | <strong>Period:</strong> 1st&ndash;4th century CE | <strong>Theism:</strong> Dualistic &mdash; the true God is hidden; the creator god (Demiurge) is lesser or evil | <strong>Proselytism:</strong> Selective &mdash; knowledge given to those deemed ready | <strong>Apostasy:</strong> Not systematically enforced</p>';
echo '<h3>Core Doctrine</h3>';
echo '<p>Gnosticism is not a single movement but a family of related religious systems sharing certain key convictions: that the material world is not the creation of the true God but of a lesser, ignorant, or malevolent creator (the Demiurge); that human beings contain a divine spark (pneuma) imprisoned in matter; and that salvation consists of <em>gnosis</em> &mdash; direct experiential knowledge of one\'s divine origin &mdash; rather than faith or works.</p>';
echo '<ul>';
echo '<li><strong>The Demiurge</strong> &mdash; the creator of the material world, identified by many Gnostic schools with the God of the Hebrew Bible. Not the true God but a lesser or fallen being who created the world in ignorance or malice.</li>';
echo '<li><strong>The Pleroma</strong> &mdash; the "Fullness" &mdash; the divine realm of light inhabited by the true God and a hierarchy of divine emanations (Aeons). The material world is a fallen reflection of the Pleroma.</li>';
echo '<li><strong>The Divine Spark</strong> &mdash; the pneuma &mdash; a fragment of divine light imprisoned in the human body. Gnosis liberates it.</li>';
echo '<li><strong>Sophia</strong> &mdash; in Sethian Gnosticism, Sophia (Wisdom) is the Aeon whose fall from the Pleroma produced the Demiurge and the material world. Her recovery is the drama of salvation.</li>';
echo '<li><strong>The Nag Hammadi Library</strong> &mdash; discovered in Egypt in 1945, this collection of 52 Gnostic texts &mdash; including the Gospel of Thomas, the Gospel of Philip, and the Apocryphon of John &mdash; transformed modern understanding of early Christian diversity. The Gospel of Thomas contains 114 sayings attributed to Jesus, many with no parallel in the canonical Gospels.</li>';
echo '</ul>';
echo '<h3>Key Text</h3>';
echo '<p><strong>Nag Hammadi Library</strong> (complete). Free: <a href="https://gnosis.org/naghamm/nhl.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">gnosis.org/naghamm/nhl.html</a></p>';

// KABBALAH
echo '<hr style="margin:28px 0;border:none;border-top:2px solid #e5e7eb;">';
echo '<h2 style="color:#2b6cb0;">&#x2295; Kabbalah</h2>';
echo '<p><strong>Origin:</strong> Provence and Spain | <strong>Period:</strong> 12th century CE (systematic formulation) | <strong>Theism:</strong> Jewish monotheism &mdash; Ein Sof (the Infinite) as the hidden God | <strong>Proselytism:</strong> Traditionally restricted to observant male Jews over 40; now more open | <strong>Apostasy:</strong> Within Jewish framework</p>';
echo '<h3>Core Doctrine</h3>';
echo '<ul>';
echo '<li><strong>Ein Sof</strong> &mdash; "Without End" &mdash; the true nature of God: infinite, unknowable, beyond all attributes. The God described in the Torah is a manifestation of Ein Sof, not Ein Sof itself.</li>';
echo '<li><strong>The Ten Sefirot</strong> &mdash; ten divine attributes or emanations through which Ein Sof manifests and through which creation occurs: Keter (Crown), Chokhmah (Wisdom), Binah (Understanding), Chesed (Loving-kindness), Gevurah (Strength), Tiferet (Beauty), Netzach (Victory), Hod (Splendor), Yesod (Foundation), Malkhut (Kingdom).</li>';
echo '<li><strong>The Tree of Life</strong> &mdash; the diagram mapping the ten Sefirot and the 22 paths connecting them &mdash; corresponding to the 22 letters of the Hebrew alphabet. The most widely used diagram in Western esoterism.</li>';
echo '<li><strong>The Zohar</strong> &mdash; the central text of Kabbalah, composed primarily by Moses de Le&oacute;n in 13th-century Spain and attributed to the 2nd-century sage Shimon bar Yochai. Written in Aramaic, it is a mystical commentary on the Torah of extraordinary density and beauty.</li>';
echo '<li><strong>The Four Worlds</strong> &mdash; Atziluth (Emanation), Beriah (Creation), Yetzirah (Formation), Assiah (Action) &mdash; four levels of reality between Ein Sof and the physical world.</li>';
echo '<li><strong>Tikkun Olam</strong> &mdash; "repair of the world" &mdash; the Lurianic Kabbalistic concept that divine sparks fell into the material world at the moment of creation (the Shevirat HaKelim &mdash; "breaking of the vessels") and that human action can collect and restore them, hastening the messianic redemption.</li>';
echo '</ul>';
echo '<h3>Key Text</h3>';
echo '<p><strong>The Zohar</strong> (selections). Free: <a href="https://sacred-texts.com/jud/index.htm" target="_blank" rel="noopener">sacred-texts.com/jud</a> &mdash; Complete: <a href="https://sefaria.org" target="_blank" rel="noopener">sefaria.org</a></p>';

// NEOPLATONISM
echo '<hr style="margin:28px 0;border:none;border-top:2px solid #e5e7eb;">';
echo '<h2 style="color:#553c9a;">&#x2295; Neoplatonism</h2>';
echo '<p><strong>Origin:</strong> Alexandria/Rome | <strong>Period:</strong> 3rd&ndash;6th century CE | <strong>Theism:</strong> Monistic &mdash; The One as the source of all reality | <strong>Proselytism:</strong> Philosophical school | <strong>Apostasy:</strong> Not applicable</p>';
echo '<h3>Core Doctrine</h3>';
echo '<ul>';
echo '<li><strong>The One</strong> &mdash; the ultimate principle, beyond being and beyond thought. Not a god who acts or thinks, but the ground from which all existence flows. Plotinus: "The One is perfect because it seeks nothing, has nothing, needs nothing."</li>';
echo '<li><strong>Emanation</strong> &mdash; from The One proceeds Nous (Divine Mind/Intellect), and from Nous proceeds Psyche (Soul), and from Soul proceeds the material world. This is not creation ex nihilo but eternal overflow &mdash; as light flows from the sun without diminishing it.</li>';
echo '<li><strong>The Return</strong> &mdash; the soul\'s path back to The One through philosophy, purification, and ultimately mystical union (<em>henosis</em>). Plotinus described achieving this union several times in his life. The goal of philosophy is not merely to understand reality but to become united with it.</li>';
echo '<li><strong>Theurgy</strong> &mdash; developed by Iamblichus: ritual operations that assist the soul\'s ascent by working with the divine powers embedded in material symbols, names, and actions. The theurgist does not command the gods but participates with them.</li>';
echo '</ul>';
echo '<h3>Significance</h3>';
echo '<p>Neoplatonism is the philosophical backbone of Western esoterism. Hermeticism, Gnosticism, Christian mysticism, Islamic philosophy, Jewish Kabbalah, and Renaissance magic all drew directly on Neoplatonic concepts. Its influence on the development of Christian theology (through Augustine and Pseudo-Dionysius) and Islamic philosophy (through Al-Farabi and Ibn Sina) makes it one of the most consequential philosophical traditions in history.</p>';
echo '<h3>Key Text</h3>';
echo '<p><strong>The Enneads</strong> by Plotinus (c. 270 CE). Free: <a href="https://sacred-texts.com/cla/plotenn/index.htm" target="_blank" rel="noopener">sacred-texts.com/cla/plotenn</a></p>';

// ALCHEMY
echo '<hr style="margin:28px 0;border:none;border-top:2px solid #e5e7eb;">';
echo '<h2 style="color:#b7791f;">&#x2295; Alchemy</h2>';
echo '<p><strong>Origin:</strong> Egypt/Greece, developed in Islamic and European contexts | <strong>Period:</strong> 1st century CE &ndash; 18th century CE | <strong>Theism:</strong> Variable &mdash; operates within Islamic, Christian, and Hermetic frameworks | <strong>Proselytism:</strong> None &mdash; transmitted master to student</p>';
echo '<h3>Core Doctrine</h3>';
echo '<p>Alchemy is simultaneously a physical practice (working with metals and substances) and a spiritual philosophy (the transformation of the soul). The two dimensions are inseparable in classical alchemy &mdash; the laboratory operations are also interior operations; the transformation of base metal into gold mirrors the transformation of the impure soul into its divine state.</p>';
echo '<ul>';
echo '<li><strong>The Philosopher\'s Stone</strong> &mdash; the substance capable of transmuting base metals into gold and conferring immortality. Its attainment is the goal of the Great Work (<em>Magnum Opus</em>). In psychological terms (Jung\'s reading), it represents the integrated, individuated self.</li>';
echo '<li><strong>The Four Stages of the Great Work</strong> &mdash; Nigredo (blackening &mdash; dissolution, putrefaction, the death of the old), Albedo (whitening &mdash; purification), Citrinitas (yellowing &mdash; the dawning of solar consciousness), Rubedo (reddening &mdash; the completed transformation, the conjunction of opposites).</li>';
echo '<li><strong>The Three Principles</strong> (Paracelsus) &mdash; Sulfur (soul, active principle), Mercury (spirit, mediating principle), Salt (body, passive principle). All matter is a combination of these three.</li>';
echo '<li><strong>Jung and Alchemy</strong> &mdash; C.G. Jung\'s <em>Psychology and Alchemy</em> (1944) demonstrated that alchemical symbolism maps onto the process of psychological individuation &mdash; the integration of the shadow, the anima/animus, and ultimately the Self. Jung considered alchemy the Western equivalent of Yoga.</li>';
echo '</ul>';

// ROSICRUCIANISM
echo '<hr style="margin:28px 0;border:none;border-top:2px solid #e5e7eb;">';
echo '<h2 style="color:#276749;">&#x2295; Rosicrucianism</h2>';
echo '<p><strong>Origin:</strong> Germany | <strong>Period:</strong> 1614&ndash;1616 CE (manifestos) | <strong>Theism:</strong> Christian esoteric | <strong>Proselytism:</strong> Initiated &mdash; one must be called | <strong>Apostasy:</strong> Not systematically enforced</p>';
echo '<h3>Core Doctrine</h3>';
echo '<p>The Rosicrucian movement began with three anonymous manifestos published in Germany between 1614 and 1616: the <em>Fama Fraternitatis</em>, the <em>Confessio Fraternitatis</em>, and <em>The Chymical Wedding of Christian Rosenkreutz</em>. They announced the existence of a secret brotherhood founded by Christian Rosenkreutz (CR), who had traveled to the East and returned with hidden wisdom. The manifestos called for a universal reformation of humanity through the combination of Hermetic philosophy, Kabbalah, alchemy, and Christian mysticism.</p>';
echo '<ul>';
echo '<li><strong>Christian Rosenkreutz</strong> &mdash; the legendary founder, said to have lived 106 years (1378&ndash;1484). His vault, rediscovered by the brotherhood, is described in elaborate symbolic detail.</li>';
echo '<li><strong>The Invisible College</strong> &mdash; the Rosicrucian brothers were said to be invisible, moving through society unrecognized, healing the sick for free. The "invisible college" concept influenced Francis Bacon\'s <em>New Atlantis</em> and the founding of the Royal Society.</li>';
echo '<li><strong>Later Orders</strong> &mdash; the original manifestos may have been a literary hoax or a serious philosophical programme &mdash; debate continues. What followed were real organizations: the Gold und Rosenkreuz (Germany, 18th century), the Societas Rosicruciana in Anglia (19th century), AMORC (20th century).</li>';
echo '</ul>';

// FREEMASONRY
echo '<hr style="margin:28px 0;border:none;border-top:2px solid #e5e7eb;">';
echo '<h2 style="color:#2b6cb0;">&#x2295; Freemasonry</h2>';
echo '<p><strong>Origin:</strong> England/Scotland | <strong>Period:</strong> 1717 CE (Grand Lodge formation) | <strong>Theism:</strong> Deistic &mdash; "the Great Architect of the Universe" | <strong>Proselytism:</strong> By invitation | <strong>Apostasy:</strong> Resignation from the Lodge</p>';
echo '<h3>Core Doctrine</h3>';
echo '<p>Freemasonry claims descent from the medieval stonemasons\' guilds and, more speculatively, from the builders of Solomon\'s Temple. Its three basic degrees &mdash; Entered Apprentice, Fellow Craft, Master Mason &mdash; use the tools and vocabulary of stonemasonry as moral and philosophical allegories.</p>';
echo '<ul>';
echo '<li><strong>The Legend of Hiram Abiff</strong> &mdash; the central Masonic myth: Hiram, the architect of Solomon\'s Temple, is murdered by three ruffians who seek to steal the Master\'s Word. His death and symbolic resurrection in the Master Mason degree encode the esoteric teaching about death, initiation, and regeneration.</li>';
echo '<li><strong>The Great Architect</strong> &mdash; Freemasonry requires belief in a Supreme Being but does not specify which one &mdash; making it compatible with Christianity, Islam, Judaism, Deism, or any theistic belief. This theological openness was deliberately designed to unite men across religious divisions.</li>';
echo '<li><strong>Political Significance</strong> &mdash; Freemasonry was the social network through which Enlightenment ideals spread. American Founding Fathers (Washington, Franklin, Jefferson &mdash; though Jefferson was not a Mason), French revolutionaries, and independence leaders across Latin America and Africa were Masons. Its emphasis on reason, tolerance, and human equality made it both the vehicle of liberal democratic revolution and the object of Catholic and authoritarian suspicion.</li>';
echo '</ul>';
echo '<h3>Key Text</h3>';
echo '<p><strong>Morals and Dogma</strong> by Albert Pike (1871). Free: <a href="https://sacred-texts.com/mas/md/index.htm" target="_blank" rel="noopener">sacred-texts.com/mas/md</a></p>';

// THEOSOPHY
echo '<hr style="margin:28px 0;border:none;border-top:2px solid #e5e7eb;">';
echo '<h2 style="color:#2d6a1f;">&#x2295; Theosophy</h2>';
echo '<p><strong>Origin:</strong> New York | <strong>Period:</strong> 1875 CE | <strong>Founder:</strong> Helena Petrovna Blavatsky (1831&ndash;1891) | <strong>Theism:</strong> Monistic panentheism | <strong>Proselytism:</strong> Open membership</p>';
echo '<h3>Core Doctrine</h3>';
echo '<ul>';
echo '<li><strong>The Secret Doctrine\'s Three Propositions</strong>: (1) An Omnipresent, Eternal, Boundless Immutable Principle beyond human conception. (2) The absolute universality of the law of periodicity &mdash; the underlying principle of reincarnation and karma. (3) The fundamental identity of all individual souls with the Universal Oversoul, and the obligatory pilgrimage for every soul through the cycle of incarnation.</li>';
echo '<li><strong>The Seven Planes</strong> &mdash; reality organized in seven interpenetrating planes: Physical, Astral, Mental, Buddhic, Atmic, Monadic, Divine.</li>';
echo '<li><strong>The Mahatmas</strong> &mdash; evolved human beings who have completed their human evolution but remain to guide humanity\'s spiritual development. Blavatsky claimed to receive teachings from two such Masters &mdash; Morya and Koot Hoomi.</li>';
echo '<li><strong>Influence</strong> &mdash; Theosophy was the first Western system to systematically synthesize Hindu, Buddhist, and Western esoteric concepts for a mass audience. It shaped Rudolf Steiner\'s Anthroposophy, Alice Bailey\'s Arcane School, and provided the conceptual vocabulary (planes, karma, chakras, Masters, Akashic Records) that permeates popular spirituality today.</li>';
echo '</ul>';
echo '<h3>Key Texts</h3>';
echo '<ul>';
echo '<li><strong>The Secret Doctrine</strong> (1888). Free: <a href="https://sacred-texts.com/the/sd/index.htm" target="_blank" rel="noopener">sacred-texts.com/the/sd</a></li>';
echo '<li><strong>Isis Unveiled</strong> (1877). Free: <a href="https://sacred-texts.com/the/iu/index.htm" target="_blank" rel="noopener">sacred-texts.com/the/iu</a></li>';
echo '</ul>';

// GOLDEN DAWN AND THELEMA
echo '<hr style="margin:28px 0;border:none;border-top:2px solid #e5e7eb;">';
echo '<h2 style="color:#553c9a;">&#x2295; The Hermetic Order of the Golden Dawn &amp; Thelema</h2>';
echo '<p><strong>Origin:</strong> England | <strong>Period:</strong> Golden Dawn 1888; Thelema 1904 | <strong>Founders:</strong> Westcott, Mathers (Golden Dawn); Aleister Crowley (Thelema) | <strong>Theism:</strong> Polytheistic/pantheistic magical system</p>';
echo '<h3>The Golden Dawn</h3>';
echo '<p>Founded in London in 1888, the Golden Dawn synthesized Kabbalah, Tarot, astrology, Enochian magic, Hermeticism, and ceremonial magic into the most sophisticated initiatory system in Western history. Its members included W.B. Yeats, Algernon Blackwood, Arthur Machen, and Aleister Crowley. Its grade system corresponded to the Kabbalistic Tree of Life.</p>';
echo '<h3>Thelema</h3>';
echo '<ul>';
echo '<li><strong>The Book of the Law</strong> &mdash; received (or composed) by Aleister Crowley in Cairo in 1904; proclaimed: "Do what thou wilt shall be the whole of the Law. Love is the law, love under will."</li>';
echo '<li><strong>True Will</strong> &mdash; every person has a True Will &mdash; their deepest, most authentic purpose &mdash; distinct from the ego\'s surface desires. The spiritual task is to discover and fulfill this Will.</li>';
echo '<li><strong>The Three Aeons</strong> &mdash; Aeon of Isis (matriarchy), Aeon of Osiris (patriarchal dying-and-rising god religions, including Christianity), Aeon of Horus (current age of individual will and self-realization).</li>';
echo '<li><strong>The Holy Guardian Angel</strong> &mdash; the supreme Thelemic magical attainment: Knowledge and Conversation of one\'s Holy Guardian Angel &mdash; contact with one\'s higher self or divine genius.</li>';
echo '</ul>';
echo '<h3>Key Texts</h3>';
echo '<ul>';
echo '<li><strong>The Book of the Law</strong> (1904). Free: <a href="https://sacred-texts.com/oto/engccxx.htm" target="_blank" rel="noopener">sacred-texts.com</a></li>';
echo '<li><strong>The Golden Dawn</strong> by Israel Regardie (1937). Free: <a href="https://archive.org/search?query=regardie+golden+dawn" target="_blank" rel="noopener">archive.org</a></li>';
echo '</ul>';

